n/17b77cd0d9885f4c7dedb3ddb65d0bcd1be660f1acf857e78dfc3a07e480dbc4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-38593" :question/id #uuid "c7d02b61-80d9-5e06-9924-32034d7e7b9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-38593" :question/problem "The number of weekly sales of a particular book decreases with time. The number of units sold at any given moment decreases at a rate that's proportional to the number of units sold at that moment. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of units sold $V(t)$ at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time since the book first went on sale?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **number of units sold** at any given moment **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to **the number of units sold** at that moment.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *number of units sold* of a particular book varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *number of units sold.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝V.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number of units sold *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-38593/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-38593"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-38591/selection/a402728d5a026d15babe92e4aa18d4defbb6d9f07404a8922ecc5514bfeab10d" :answer/id #uuid "bc3878d5-5ebd